<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juan Manuel Rodríguez</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1771">1771</a>–<a href="/years/1837">1837</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juan Manuel Rodríguez was a Salvadoran independence leader who took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a> uprising of 1811. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> left the Mexican Empire, he became the first head of state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a> within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America</a> in 1824. His government abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in the federation that year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-manuel-rodriguez.jpg" alt="Juan Manuel Rodríguez" width="320" height="449" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Elfather503 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juan_Manuel_Rodr%C3%ADguez,_political_chief,_independence_leader_and_founding_father_of_Central_America.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Juan Manuel Rodríguez was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a> on 31 December 1771, the son of Pedro Delgado and Josefa Rodríguez. With Father José Matías Delgado and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-jose-arce">Manuel José Arce</a> he was one of the chief leaders of the independence movements of 1811 and 1814, and he served as secretary of the junta that governed the province as its first independent government in November 1811.</p><p>Elected mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a> in 1814, he supported the insurrection of 24 January 1814. When it was put down he was arrested and sentenced to six years in prison, but he was pardoned in 1819.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In November 1821 he was appointed to the governing body of the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>, and in January 1822 he signed the act of separation from the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, opposing incorporation into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-mexican-empire">First Mexican Empire</a>. He was then sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, to negotiate the possible admission of El Salvador to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> as a protection against Mexican expansion, an initiative that ended with the fall of the empire in 1823.</p><p>On 22 April 1824 the constituent assembly elected him the first head of state, and on the next day he formally enacted the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. His administration decreed the founding of the Diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-matias-delgado">José Matías Delgado</a> as its first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. In May 1824 he oversaw the publication and swearing-in of the first state constitution, set up the Supreme Court of Justice and organised the first constitutional elections, won by Juan Vicente Villacorta Díaz as head of state and Mariano Prado as his deputy. The first official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press began work that June, and the state's first newspaper, El Semanario Político Mercantil, appeared on 31 July 1824. On 1 October 1824 he handed executive power to Prado.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After further service, including a term as Treasury Secretary, Rodríguez retired to his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hacienda">hacienda</a> of San Jerónimo near Cojutepeque. In his last years he took minor orders with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> and gave himself to charitable works, and he died of cholera. He is regarded as a founding father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a> for his part in the movements for independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-mexican-empire">First Mexican Empire</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Juan Manuel Rodríguez” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>With the creation of the Federal Republic of Central America, El Salvador became one of its five states under its own constitution of 1824, with <b>Juan Manuel Rodríguez</b> as first head of state.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-el-salvador">State of El Salvador</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>. Died in Cojutepeque, in what is now <a href="/countries/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=13.7,-89.19&amp;year=1813" aria-label="San Salvador on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2106 1654 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Juan Manuel Rodríguez"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2556 1924h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2556 1924h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=13.7,-89.19&amp;year=1813">What was here in 1813? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
